  "title": "Chapter 48: Budget and Taxation in India",
  "order": 48,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "ckkyxqj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Budget"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Constitution of India uses the term 'Annual Financial Statement' under Article 112 instead of the word 'Budget'.",
        "The Government Budget is split into two main parts: Revenue Budget (running expenses and recurring receipts) and Capital Budget (assets and liabilities).",
        "Direct taxes are non-shiftable and progressive, whereas indirect taxes are shiftable to consumers and destination-based.",
        "GST replaced multiple central and state indirect taxes, creating a unified common national market under the 101st Constitutional Amendment.",
        "Fiscal Deficit represents the total borrowing requirements of the government during a financial year."
      ],
      "id": "607gxzd"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Constitutional Provision",
          "value": "Article 112",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Financial Year Period",
          "value": "April 1 - March 31",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "GST Implementation",
          "value": "July 1, 2017",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "GST Constitutional Act",
